--- body ---
## Context

Child 2 of tracker `tickets/0240`, which owns the rationale. The tracker's
explicit requirement: adding a model later costs one line and a run, not a
rewrite.

Two measurement disciplines were learned the hard way and are requirements, not
preferences — warm runs only, and repeats with a reported spread. The tracker
records what a cold run cost and what the warm spread is; cite it rather than
repeating the figures.

This ticket ships no measurements. It ships the thing that takes them, plus the
tests that say it is taking the right ones.

## Relevant files

- `tickets/0240-...erg` — the tracker
- the registry from `tickets/0261`
- `bench/query_embed_cost.mjs` — RAM, latency, batch 1
- `bench/quant_fidelity.mjs` — vectors at a rung
- `bench/quant_fidelity_score.py` — fidelity and rank agreement
- `bench/merge_dtype_ladder.py` — cost ladder
- `bench/vec_task_recall.mjs` — recall
- the scratch `sweep.sh` / `fidsweep.sh` pattern, to be promoted into `bench/`

## Actions

1. Promote the scratch sweep scripts into `bench/` as one registry-driven runner.
   Reuse the five drivers above; do not write a sixth thing that overlaps them.
2. Key each result file on `(model, dtype, device, corpus, driver version)`, one
   file per cell, written incrementally. A partial sweep is a valid state.
3. Make it idempotent and resumable: a completed cell is skipped, an absent cell
   is run. The runs are long and will be interrupted.
4. One process per cell, so RSS is what a fresh server pays rather than a delta
   against a warmed allocator.
5. Download first, then measure. Never let a cold run enter a table.
6. Repeat every RSS cell over five fresh processes; report median with spread.
7. Hash the resolved ONNX file per cell. On a collision with a cell already
   measured, record the cell as a duplicate rather than spending a run on it.
8. Apply the registry's per-model input template in the driver, and record it in
   every result file.
9. Record the four engine versions and the device actually selected — not the
   device requested — in every result file.
10. Make the scorer read what exists: report a missing cell as missing and score
    the rest, rather than failing or silently reporting a short table.

## Test

Written before the campaign, per the tracker:

- A cell already present is skipped and NOT recomputed; a cell absent IS run.
  Assert both directions — "skips everything" and "resumes correctly" look
  identical on a full directory. This is the positive control that makes the
  skip test a check rather than a null.
- The scorer reports a missing cell as missing and still scores the rest.
- Positive control on the fidelity scorer: fp32 against itself returns cosine
  1,0 and overlap 1,0.
- A duplicate ONNX hash is recorded as a duplicate, and no second run is spent.
  Drive it with two registry entries pointing at one known-identical file.
- A recorded engine-version mismatch between a result file and the running
  environment is surfaced, not ignored.
- A model unloadable at a dtype is recorded as a result, not as an absent cell —
  the two must not serialise the same way.

## Verification

- [ ] Adding one candidate to the registry and re-running measures only the new
      cells; demonstrate it by adding one
- [ ] `make check` green
- [ ] No model id appears in `bench/` outside the registry

## Invariants

- The registry stays the only place a model name appears
- Existing artifacts in `bench/results/0025-x1-recall/` are not deleted or
  rewritten
- R20's RSS gate is NOT loosened or disabled to make a run pass — it goes red for
  the right reason and is re-pinned only in `tickets/0268`

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Registry-driven resumable harness in `bench/`, with every test above
- [ ] The five existing drivers are reused, not duplicated
- [ ] A demonstrated resume: one added candidate measures only its own cells
